<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">TABLE II</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">The experiment results of various MLLMS on the proposed Re-attack method.<math id="S5.T2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="+" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>+</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">+</annotation></semantics></math> represents the ASR change compared to the +Opt image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> setting.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span> </span><span id="S5.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Evaluation Without the </span><span id="S5.SS2.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span><span id="S5.SS2.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Strategy</span></h3>

<div id="S5.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we will show the performance of our proposed text and image prompt over the HADES attack with an example ((3)Privacy dataset on LLaVA-1.6-mistral-7b model.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>1 </span>Evaluation Settings</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To verify the effectiveness of the proposed method, we consider the below six evaluation settings:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+Opt image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></span>: The black-box version of HADES, including <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 1</span> and <span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Step 2</span> described in Section III.A. We utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian blur</span>: We utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1). The size of Gaussian kernel is chosen as 2 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">40</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Colorjitter</span>: We utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Color}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Color}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1). The setting of colorjitter follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">41</a>]</cite> with brightness=0.5 and contrast=0.5.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Posterization</span>: We utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Poster}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Poster}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1). The setting of the random posterization follows <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> with bits=2.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposed text prompt</span>: We utilize the proposed text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> and image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposed(both)</span>:We utilize the proposed text prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> and proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS2.SSS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math>, which bring the best performance among the proposed mutation method.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-B</span>2 </span>Evaluation Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The evaluation result in Table 1 demonstrates that both our proposed image prompt (mutation method) and text prompt can achieve better ASR result over the HADES attack. In particular, among the three mutation method, the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Gaussian Blur</span> (83.44<math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>) will generate the most harmful responses. However, for both the proposed image prompt and text prompt, the ASR improvement over the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+Opt image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></span> setting is quite low, with maximum 2.22<math id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>. If we read through the response results carefully, we can find that some jailbreak cases can be successfully attacked by HADES but fail when using the proposed method. The examples are shown in Fig. <a href="#S4.F4" title="Fig. 4 ‣ IV-C Proposed Re-attack Strategy ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. We can find that while the proposed image prompt brings reject response, the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposed text prompt</span> just outputs the failure response relating to the description about the image. It motivates us to propose the <span id="S5.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span> strategy, which utilizes the HADES image and text prompt during the initial attack and proposed image and text prompt during the re-attack to solve the failure HADES cases and further improve the ASR result.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S5.SS3.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span> </span><span id="S5.SS3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Main Experiments</span></h3>

<section id="S5.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span>1 </span>Evaluation Settings</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To verify the effectiveness of the proposed method, we consider the below five evaluation settings. For all the four types Re-attack setting, we utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> of HADES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> during the initial attack:</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+Opt image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></span>: The details follow Section V.B.1). It is utilized as the baseline attack method for all the MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(image)</span>: During the re-attack, we utilize the text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> and the proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1) for all the MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(multi-image)</span>:During the re-attack, we utilize the proposed text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Multi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Multi}</annotation></semantics></math> and multi-image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Multi}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Multi}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.2) for the DeepSeek-Vl2-small model and GPT-4o model.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(text)</span>:During the re-attack, we utilize the proposed text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Proposed Text Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> and the image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> for all the MLLMs.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(both)</span>:During the re-attack, we utilize the proposed best text prompt and image prompt. In the DeepSeek-Vl2-small model and GPT-4o model, the best text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Multi}-T_{Opt}+T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Multi}-T_{Opt}+T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proposed one from Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Proposed Text Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> and Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.2), and the best image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Multi}+I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Multi}+I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proposed one from Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1 and 2). For other MLLMs models, the best text prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proposed one from Section <a href="#S4.SS1" title="IV-A Proposed Text Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-A</span></a> and the best image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS1.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math> is the proposed one from Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="IV-B Proposed Image Prompt ‣ IV Proposed Jailbreaking Methodology ‣ Enhanced MLLM Black-Box Jailbreaking Attacks and Defenses" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">IV-B</span></a>.1).</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">V-C</span>2 </span>Experiment Results</h4>

<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">As shown in Table II, the proposed <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span> method significantly boosts the ASR across both open- and closed-source MLLMs. Similar to the findings of HADES <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite>, the harmful instructions related to Financial, Privacy, and Violence are more likely to bypass the model’s safeguard when examining the model’s performance across various categories.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the open-source MLLMs: under the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">+Opt image <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite></span> setting, varied attack results are illustrated among different models. The original HADES can only work well on the LLaVA models with the average ASR results above 60<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>. The incorporation of the proposed image prompt <math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{Gau}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{Gau}</annotation></semantics></math> under the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(image)</span> setting can improve the average ASR results for all the models, ranging from 8.75<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the LLaVA-1.6-mistral-7b to 16.14<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> for the MiniGPT-v2. These results further support our previous empirical observations: The <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span> strategy can resolve failed HADES cases and increase the ASR results. For the DeepSeek-Vl2-small model, the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(multi-image)</span> setting can increase additional average 28.18<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> ASR compared to the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(image)</span> setting. It illustrates the multi-image has higher possibility to break through the model’s safeguards than the single image. Moreover, the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(text)</span> setting can dramatically improve the ASR results with more than 15<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all models, even more than 40 percent (<em id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">e.g.</em>, DeepSeek-Vl2-small(41.42<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>)). Finally. the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(both)</span> setting achieves the best ASR results among all the settings. In particular, it can achieve average ASR of over 70<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> for all the models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the closed-source MLLM: GPT-4o shows the greatest resistance to HADES, leading to a 3.07<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math> of harmful responses. The OpenAI has applied external red teaming and might identify signs of typographic attacks by analyzing the special string characteristics in the text prompt <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">46</a>]</cite>. Hence, different from the results in the open-source MLLMs, the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(text)</span> setting can only show similar average ASR result (3.31<math id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>%</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\%</annotation></semantics></math>) as the HADES. Among all the settings, the <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack(multi-image)</span> setting achieves the best attack result with around 4.6 times ASR improvement. Therefore even though commercial models such as GPT-4o have stronger security alignment, multi-image attacks still pose a considerable threat.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Additional jailbreak scenarios where the HADES attack <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> fails but our proposed <span id="S5.SS3.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span> method succeeds will be provided in the APPENDIX.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VI </span><span id="S6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Proposed Defense Methodology</span></h2>

<section id="S6.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VI-A</span> </span><span id="S6.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Enhanced AdaShield Defense</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the original AdaShield (AdaShield-S and AdaShield-A) defense <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, the final text prompt can be illustrated as:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.E4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{text}=T_{attack}+T_{defense}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{text}=T_{attack}+T_{defense}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Here the <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{attack}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{attack}</annotation></semantics></math> is the text query, which can be chosen from the HADES text prompt <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Opt}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Opt}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> or proposed text prompt <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{Pro}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>r</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{Pro}</annotation></semantics></math> in Section IV.A, and <math id="S6.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{defense}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{defense}</annotation></semantics></math> is the defense prompt generated by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">42</a>]</cite>, the findings show that MLLM will take the order of input text into account and tend to assign greater attention weights to earlier inputs, thereby making them more influential on the model’s output. In order to let the MLLM focus more on the defense prompt, we propose the enhanced AdaShield defense by modifying the sequence of the text prompt. The proposed final text prompt for the enhanced AdaShield defense will be:</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<table id="S6.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T_{text}=T_{defense}+T_{attack}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>x</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T_{text}=T_{defense}+T_{attack}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S6.SS2.4" class="ltx_text">VI-B</span> </span><span id="S6.SS2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Enhanced JailGuard Defense</span></h3>

<div id="S6.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For the JailGuard <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> defense, a development set consisting of 70% of the total JailGuard dataset is utilized to measure the detection results under threshold ranging from 0.001 to 1. Then the build-in detection threshold <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> can be chosen based on the overall detection results for the MiniGPT-4 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite> model. However, the build-in <math id="S6.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\theta" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>θ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\theta</annotation></semantics></math> will be biased for a different dataset or MLLM model and this approach will require high workload to choose the detection threshold for different datasets and MLLM models.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">43</a>]</cite>, the detection threshold can be set under the FRR allowance with few-shot data (<math id="S6.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="K=16" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">K=16</annotation></semantics></math> samples per class) for various datasets. In order to choose the detection threshold for various MLLM models more effectively, we propose to use the few-shot data as a development set and set the FRR allowance on the development set to choose the detection threshold for various MLLM models.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S7" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">VII </span><span id="S7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Experiment for the Proposed Defense Methodology</span></h2>

<section id="S7.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection"><span id="S7.SS1.4" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span> </span><span id="S7.SS1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Experiment Settings</span></h3>

<section id="S7.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS1.4" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>1 </span>Models</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use three open-soured MLLMs to evaluate the proposed attack method over the defense methods <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>, <a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">39</a>]</cite> and their enhanced version.</p>
</div>
<div id="S7.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S7.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S7.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1 LLaVA-NeXT model: LLaVA-1.6-vicuna-7b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">7</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">1 MiniGPT model: MiniGPT-4-vicuna-7b <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">11</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S7.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S7.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">DeepSeek-Vl2-small model <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S7.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection"><span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.4" class="ltx_text">VII-A</span>2 </span>Dataset</h4>

<div id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To evaluate the original HADES attack and our propose <span id="S7.SS1.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Re-attack</span> method over the defense methods, we also adopt the HADES dataset <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib34" title="" class="ltx_ref">34</a>]</cite> introduced in Section V.A.2) and modify for the proposed method.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S7.T3" class="ltx_table">
<div id="S7.T3.2" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:603.8pt;height:160pt;vertical-align:-77.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(0.0pt,0.0pt) scale(1,1) ;">
<p id="S7.T3.2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S7.T3.2.1.1" class="ltx_text">

<span id="S7.T3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
